1
00:00:06,200 --> 00:00:09,840
A program is just a series of instructions in memory.

2
00:00:09,840 --> 00:00:17,566
The CPU remembers the address of the current instruction in a special register, the program counter, or PC.

3
00:00:17,566 --> 00:00:23,707
Every instruction takes four bytes, so after finishing one, the PC adds four and points to the next.

4
00:00:23,707 --> 00:00:29,296
But if we could only run instructions one after another, we couldn't write an if or a loop.

5
00:00:29,296 --> 00:00:32,600
We need instructions that can jump.

6
00:00:32,600 --> 00:00:39,782
The conditional branch beq, which stands for branch if equal, jumps to the label if rs1 equals rs2,

7
00:00:39,782 --> 00:00:43,833
and otherwise carries on with the next instruction.

8
00:00:43,833 --> 00:00:50,225
RISC-V has six conditional branches, which are equal, not equal, less than, greater than or equal,

9
00:00:50,225 --> 00:00:54,464
and the unsigned versions of less than and greater than or equal.

10
00:00:54,464 --> 00:01:00,465
There is no bgt instruction and no ble instruction, because we can simply swap the operands,

11
00:01:00,465 --> 00:01:04,117
since a is greater than b exactly when b is less than a.

12
00:01:04,117 --> 00:01:09,133
The assembler also offers pseudo-instructions for these.

13
00:01:09,133 --> 00:01:14,251
There is also an unconditional jump, j Label, which jumps straight there.

14
00:01:14,251 --> 00:01:19,229
It is shorthand for jal x0, Label, and we'll meet jal in episode seven.

15
00:01:19,229 --> 00:01:27,200
A label is just a name for a position in the code, and the assembler converts it into an address.

16
00:01:27,200 --> 00:01:29,416
Let's translate an if-else.

17
00:01:29,416 --> 00:01:34,200
The variables f, g, h, i, and j live in s0 through s4.

18
00:01:34,200 --> 00:01:36,908
The key trick is inverting the condition.

19
00:01:36,908 --> 00:01:39,550
In C the then part runs when i equals j.

20
00:01:39,550 --> 00:01:42,522
So in assembly we turn it around and use bne,

21
00:01:42,522 --> 00:01:48,300
which skips the then part when the values are not equal and goes straight to Else.

22
00:01:48,300 --> 00:01:50,335
Case one is when i equals j.

23
00:01:50,335 --> 00:01:57,966
The bne doesn't jump, so we execute the add, and then j Exit jumps over the else part.

24
00:01:57,966 --> 00:02:00,316
Case two is when i does not equal j.

25
00:02:00,316 --> 00:02:06,126
The bne jumps straight to Else, we execute the sub, and then we naturally arrive at Exit.

26
00:02:06,126 --> 00:02:08,802
Notice that the j Exit can't be left out,

27
00:02:08,802 --> 00:02:16,100
because otherwise the program would fall right through into the else part after the then part.

28
00:02:16,100 --> 00:02:23,233
Before writing loops, let's meet a few bitwise instructions, which work on thirty-two bit data one bit at a time.

29
00:02:23,233 --> 00:02:28,947
The and instruction gives a one only when both bits are one, and it is often used as a mask,

30
00:02:28,947 --> 00:02:30,811
keeping just the bits we want.

31
00:02:30,811 --> 00:02:34,165
The or instruction gives a one when either bit is one,

32
00:02:34,165 --> 00:02:38,766
and the xor instruction gives a one only when the two bits differ.

33
00:02:38,766 --> 00:02:47,235
They all come in immediate versions, andi, ori, and xori, so andi t0, t1, 0xFF extracts the lowest byte.

34
00:02:47,235 --> 00:02:50,086
RISC-V has no real not instruction.

35
00:02:50,086 --> 00:03:00,166
Minus one has every bit set, so xor with it flips every bit, which is how the not pseudo-instruction works.

36
00:03:00,166 --> 00:03:03,827
The shift instructions move all the bits over together.

37
00:03:03,827 --> 00:03:11,150
The sll instruction is a logical left shift, which moves the bits to the left and fills in zeros on the right.

38
00:03:11,150 --> 00:03:15,610
Shifting left by k bits is the same as multiplying by two to the k,

39
00:03:15,610 --> 00:03:20,633
so twenty-two shifted left by two becomes eighty-eight.

40
00:03:20,633 --> 00:03:23,009
There are two kinds of right shift.

41
00:03:23,009 --> 00:03:28,168
The srl instruction is a logical right shift, which fills zeros on the left.

42
00:03:28,168 --> 00:03:34,141
The sra instruction is an arithmetic right shift, which fills in copies of the sign bit,

43
00:03:34,141 --> 00:03:39,933
so a negative number stays negative after division by powers of two.

44
00:03:39,933 --> 00:03:45,683
Now let's put all this together, to add up the n elements of an array A.

45
00:03:45,683 --> 00:03:51,966
The address of A is in a0 and n is in a1, while i uses t0 and sum uses s1.

46
00:03:51,966 --> 00:03:53,982
Here is the skeleton of the loop.

47
00:03:53,982 --> 00:03:57,708
At the start we check the condition and jump out if it fails,

48
00:03:57,708 --> 00:04:02,168
and at the end of the body we jump back to the beginning unconditionally.

49
00:04:02,168 --> 00:04:07,238
The condition is inverted again, since the C loop continues while i is less than n,

50
00:04:07,238 --> 00:04:11,700
but the assembly jumps out when i is greater than or equal to n.

51
00:04:11,700 --> 00:04:19,110
The address of A[i] is A plus four times i, so we use slli to shift i left by two bits to get four i,

52
00:04:19,110 --> 00:04:21,833
and then add the base address.

53
00:04:21,833 --> 00:04:24,116
In the first round, i is zero.

54
00:04:24,116 --> 00:04:32,533
The bge doesn't branch, we compute the address 0x100, we load A[0], which is three, and sum becomes three.

55
00:04:32,533 --> 00:04:34,963
Every round after that is the same.

56
00:04:34,963 --> 00:04:40,797
We compute A plus four i, load A[i] and add it into sum, and then increase i by one.

57
00:04:40,797 --> 00:04:48,992
When i reaches four, the bge condition holds and we jump to Done, with sum equal to three plus one plus four plus one,

58
00:04:48,992 --> 00:04:50,833
which is nine.

